@node Introduction
@section Introduction

This is a small Ruby tutorial that should take no more than 20 minutes
to complete. It makes the assumption that you already have Ruby
installed. (If you do not have Ruby on your computer install it before
you get started.)

@node Interactive Ruby
@section Interactive Ruby

Ruby comes with a program that will show the results of any Ruby
statements you feed it. Playing with Ruby code in interactive sessions
like this is a terrific way to learn the language.

Open up IRB (which stands for Interactive Ruby).

@itemize
@item
If you're using @strong{macOS} open up @code{Terminal} and type
@code{irb}, then hit enter.
@item
If you're using @strong{Linux}, open up a shell and type @code{irb} and
hit enter.
@item
If you're using @strong{Windows}, open @code{Interactive Ruby} from the
Ruby section of your Start Menu. ^
@end itemize

@verbatim
irb(main):001:0>
@end verbatim

Ok, so it's open. Now what?

Type this: @code{"Hello World"}

@verbatim
irb(main):001:0> "Hello World"
=> "Hello World"
@end verbatim

@node Ruby Obeyed You
@section Ruby Obeyed You

What just happened? Did we just write the world's shortest ``Hello
World'' program? Not exactly. The second line is just IRB's way of
telling us the result of the last expression it evaluated. If we want to
print out ``Hello World'' we need a bit more:

@verbatim
irb(main):002:0> puts "Hello World"
Hello World
=> nil
@end verbatim

@code{puts} is the basic command to print something out in Ruby. But
then what's the @code{=> nil} bit? That's the result of the expression.
@code{puts} always returns nil, which is Ruby's
absolutely-positively-nothing value.

@node Your Free Calculator is Here
@section Your Free Calculator is Here

Already, we have enough to use IRB as a basic calculator:

@verbatim
irb(main):003:0> 3+2
=> 5
@end verbatim

Three plus two. Easy enough. What about three @emph{times} two? You
could type it in, it's short enough, but you may also be able to go up
and change what you just entered. Try hitting the @strong{up-arrow} on
your keyboard and see if it brings up the line with @code{3+2} on it. If
it does, you can use the left arrow key to move just after the @code{+}
sign and then use backspace to change it to a @code{*} sign.

@verbatim
irb(main):004:0> 3*2
=> 6
@end verbatim

Next, let's try three squared:

@verbatim
irb(main):005:0> 3**2
=> 9
@end verbatim

In Ruby @code{**} is the way you say ``to the power of''. But what if
you want to go the other way and find the square root of something?

@verbatim
irb(main):006:0> Math.sqrt(9)
=> 3.0
@end verbatim

Ok, wait, what was that last one? If you guessed, ``it was figuring out
the square root of nine,'' you're right. But let's take a closer look at
things. First of all, what's @code{Math}?

@node Modules Group Code by Topic
@section Modules Group Code by Topic

@code{Math} is a built-in module for mathematics. Modules serve two
roles in Ruby. This shows one role: grouping similar methods together
under a familiar name. @code{Math} also contains methods like
@code{sin()} and @code{tan()}.

Next is a dot. What does the dot do? The dot is how you identify the
receiver of a message. What's the message? In this case it's
@code{sqrt(9)}, which means call the method @code{sqrt}, shorthand for
``square root'' with the parameter of @code{9}.

The result of this method call is the value @code{3.0}. You might notice
it's not just @code{3}. That's because most of the time the square root
of a number won't be an integer, so the method always returns a
floating-point number.

What if we want to remember the result of some of this math? Assign the
result to a variable.

@verbatim
irb(main):007:0> a = 3 ** 2
=> 9
irb(main):008:0> b = 4 ** 2
=> 16
irb(main):009:0> Math.sqrt(a+b)
=> 5.0
@end verbatim

As great as this is for a calculator, we're getting away from the
traditional @code{Hello World} message that beginning tutorials are
supposed to focus on@dots{} so let's go back to that.

@node Step 2
@chapter Step 2

What if we want to say ``Hello'' a lot without getting our fingers all
tired? We need to define a method!

@verbatim
irb(main):010:0> def hi
irb(main):011:1> puts "Hello World!"
irb(main):012:1> end
=> :hi
@end verbatim

The code @code{def hi} starts the definition of the method. It tells
Ruby that we're defining a method, that its name is @code{hi}. The next
line is the body of the method, the same line we saw earlier:
@code{puts "Hello World"}. Finally, the last line @code{end} tells Ruby
we're done defining the method. Ruby's response @code{=> :hi} tells us
that it knows we're done defining the method. This response could be
@code{=> nil} for Ruby 2.0 and earlier versions. But, it's not important
here, so let's go on.

@menu
* The Brief Repetitive Lives of a Method::
* Holding Spots in a String::
* Evolving Into a Greeter::
@end menu

@node The Brief Repetitive Lives of a Method
@section The Brief, Repetitive Lives of a Method

Now let's try running that method a few times:

@verbatim
irb(main):013:0> hi
Hello World!
=> nil
irb(main):014:0> hi()
Hello World!
=> nil
@end verbatim

Well, that was easy. Calling a method in Ruby is as easy as just
mentioning its name to Ruby. If the method doesn't take parameters
that's all you need. You can add empty parentheses if you'd like, but
they're not needed.

What if we want to say hello to one person, and not the whole world?
Just redefine @code{hi} to take a name as a parameter.

@verbatim
irb(main):015:0> def hi(name)
irb(main):016:1> puts "Hello #{name}!"
irb(main):017:1> end
=> :hi
irb(main):018:0> hi("Matz")
Hello Matz!
=> nil
@end verbatim

So it works@dots{} but let's take a second to see what's going on here.

@node Holding Spots in a String
@section Holding Spots in a String

What's the @code{#@{name@}} bit? That's Ruby's way of inserting
something into a string. The bit between the braces is turned into a
string (if it isn't one already) and then substituted into the outer
string at that point. You can also use this to make sure that someone's
name is properly capitalized:

@verbatim
irb(main):019:0> def hi(name = "World")
irb(main):020:1> puts "Hello #{name.capitalize}!"
irb(main):021:1> end
=> :hi
irb(main):022:0> hi "chris"
Hello Chris!
=> nil
irb(main):023:0> hi
Hello World!
=> nil
@end verbatim

A couple of other tricks to spot here. One is that we're calling the
method without parentheses again. If it's obvious what you're doing, the
parentheses are optional. The other trick is the default parameter
@code{World}. What this is saying is ``If the name isn't supplied, use
the default name of @code{"World"}''.

@node Evolving Into a Greeter
@section Evolving Into a Greeter
What if we want a real greeter around, one that remembers your name and
welcomes you and treats you always with respect. You might want to use
an object for that. Let's create a ``Greeter'' class.

@verbatim
irb(main):024:0> class Greeter
irb(main):025:1>   def initialize(name = "World")
irb(main):026:2>     @name = name
irb(main):027:2>   end
irb(main):028:1>   def say_hi
irb(main):029:2>     puts "Hi #{@name}!"
irb(main):030:2>   end
irb(main):031:1>   def say_bye
irb(main):032:2>     puts "Bye #{@name}, come back soon."
irb(main):033:2>   end
irb(main):034:1> end
=> :say_bye
@end verbatim

The new keyword here is @code{class}. This defines a new class called
Greeter and a bunch of methods for that class. Also notice
@code{@@name}. This is an instance variable, and is available to all the
methods of the class. As you can see it's used by @code{say_hi} and
@code{say_bye}.

So how do we get this Greeter class set in motion? Create an object.

@node Step 3
@chapter Step 3

Now let's create a greeter object and use it:

@verbatim
irb(main):035:0> greeter = Greeter.new("Pat")
=> #<Greeter:0x16cac @name="Pat">
irb(main):036:0> greeter.say_hi
Hi Pat!
=> nil
irb(main):037:0> greeter.say_bye
Bye Pat, come back soon.
=> nil
@end verbatim

Once the @code{greeter} object is created, it remembers that the name is
Pat. Hmm, what if we want to get at the name directly?

@verbatim
irb(main):038:0> greeter.@name
SyntaxError: (irb):38: syntax error, unexpected tIVAR, expecting '('
@end verbatim

Nope, can't do it.

