@node Under the Object's Skin
@section Under the Object's Skin

Instance variables are hidden away inside the object. They're not
terribly hidden, you see them whenever you inspect the object, and there
are other ways of accessing them, but Ruby uses the good object-oriented
approach of keeping data sort-of hidden away.

So what methods do exist for Greeter objects?

@verbatim
irb(main):039:0> Greeter.instance_methods
=> [:say_hi, :say_bye, :instance_of?, :public_send,
    :instance_variable_get, :instance_variable_set,
    :instance_variable_defined?, :remove_instance_variable,
    :private_methods, :kind_of?, :instance_variables, :tap,
    :is_a?, :extend, :define_singleton_method, :to_enum,
    :enum_for, :<=>, :===, :=~, :!~, :eql?, :respond_to?,
    :freeze, :inspect, :display, :send, :object_id, :to_s,
    :method, :public_method, :singleton_method, :nil?, :hash,
    :class, :singleton_class, :clone, :dup, :itself, :taint,
    :tainted?, :untaint, :untrust, :trust, :untrusted?, :methods,
    :protected_methods, :frozen?, :public_methods, :singleton_methods,
    :!, :==, :!=, :__send__, :equal?, :instance_eval, :instance_exec, :__id__]
@end verbatim

Whoa. That's a lot of methods. We only defined two methods. What's going
on here? Well this is @strong{all} of the methods for Greeter objects, a
complete list, including ones defined by ancestor classes. If we want to
just list methods defined for Greeter we can tell it to not include
ancestors by passing it the parameter @code{false}, meaning we don't
want methods defined by ancestors.

@verbatim
irb(main):040:0> Greeter.instance_methods(false)
=> [:say_hi, :say_bye]
@end verbatim

Ah, that's more like it. So let's see which methods our greeter object
responds to:

@verbatim
irb(main):041:0> greeter.respond_to?("name")
=> false
irb(main):042:0> greeter.respond_to?("say_hi")
=> true
irb(main):043:0> greeter.respond_to?("to_s")
=> true
@end verbatim

So, it knows @code{say_hi}, and @code{to_s} (meaning convert something
to a string, a method that's defined by default for every object), but
it doesn't know @code{name}.

@node Altering Classes---It's Never Too Late
@section Altering Classes---It's Never Too Late

But what if you want to be able to view or change the name? Ruby
provides an easy way of providing access to an object's variables.

@verbatim
irb(main):044:0> class Greeter
irb(main):045:1>   attr_accessor :name
irb(main):046:1> end
=> nil
@end verbatim

In Ruby, you can open a class up again and modify it. The changes will
be present in any new objects you create and even available in existing
objects of that class. So, let's create a new object and play with its
@code{@@name} property.

@verbatim
irb(main):047:0> greeter = Greeter.new("Andy")
=> #<Greeter:0x3c9b0 @name="Andy">
irb(main):048:0> greeter.respond_to?("name")
=> true
irb(main):049:0> greeter.respond_to?("name=")
=> true
irb(main):050:0> greeter.say_hi
Hi Andy!
=> nil
irb(main):051:0> greeter.name="Betty"
=> "Betty"
irb(main):052:0> greeter
=> #<Greeter:0x3c9b0 @name="Betty">
irb(main):053:0> greeter.name
=> "Betty"
irb(main):054:0> greeter.say_hi
Hi Betty!
=> nil
@end verbatim

Using @code{attr_accessor} defined two new methods for us, @code{name}
to get the value, and @code{name=} to set it.

@node Greeting Anything and Everything MegaGreeter Neglects None
@section Greeting Anything and Everything, MegaGreeter Neglects None

This greeter isn't all that interesting though, it can only deal with
one person at a time. What if we had some kind of MegaGreeter that could
either greet the world, one person, or a whole list of people?

Let's write this one in a file instead of directly in the interactive
Ruby interpreter IRB.

To quit IRB, type ``quit'', ``exit'' or just hit Control-D.

@verbatim
#!/usr/bin/env ruby

class MegaGreeter
  attr_accessor :names

  # Create the object
  def initialize(names = "World")
    @names = names
  end

  # Say hi to everybody
  def say_hi
    if @names.nil?
      puts "..."
    elsif @names.respond_to?("each")
      # @names is a list of some kind, iterate!
      @names.each do |name|
        puts "Hello #{name}!"
      end
    else
      puts "Hello #{@names}!"
    end
  end

  # Say bye to everybody
  def say_bye
    if @names.nil?
      puts "..."
    elsif @names.respond_to?("join")
      # Join the list elements with commas
      puts "Goodbye #{@names.join(", ")}.  Come back soon!"
    else
      puts "Goodbye #{@names}.  Come back soon!"
    end
  end
end


if __FILE__ == $0
  mg = MegaGreeter.new
  mg.say_hi
  mg.say_bye

  # Change name to be "Zeke"
  mg.names = "Zeke"
  mg.say_hi
  mg.say_bye

  # Change the name to an array of names
  mg.names = ["Albert", "Brenda", "Charles",
              "Dave", "Engelbert"]
  mg.say_hi
  mg.say_bye

  # Change to nil
  mg.names = nil
  mg.say_hi
  mg.say_bye
end
@end verbatim

Save this file as ``ri20min.rb'', and run it as ``ruby ri20min.rb''. The
output should be:

@verbatim
Hello World!
Goodbye World.  Come back soon!
Hello Zeke!
Goodbye Zeke.  Come back soon!
Hello Albert!
Hello Brenda!
Hello Charles!
Hello Dave!
Hello Engelbert!
Goodbye Albert, Brenda, Charles, Dave, Engelbert.  Come
back soon!
...
...
@end verbatim

There are a lot of new things thrown into this final example that we can
take a deeper look at.

So, looking deeper at our new program, notice the initial lines, which
begin with a hash mark (#). In Ruby, anything on a line after a hash
mark is a comment and is ignored by the interpreter. The first line of
the file is a special case, and under a Unix-like operating system tells
the shell how to run the file. The rest of the comments are there just
for clarity.

Our @code{say_hi} method has become a bit trickier:

@verbatim
# Say hi to everybody
def say_hi
  if @names.nil?
    puts "..."
  elsif @names.respond_to?("each")
    # @names is a list of some kind, iterate!
    @names.each do |name|
      puts "Hello #{name}!"
    end
  else
    puts "Hello #{@names}!"
  end
end
@end verbatim

It now looks at the @code{@@names} instance variable to make decisions.
If it's nil, it just prints out three dots. No point greeting nobody,
right?

@node Cycling and Looping---aka Iteration
@section Cycling and Looping---a.k.a. Iteration

If the @code{@@names} object responds to @code{each}, it is something
that you can iterate over, so iterate over it and greet each person in
turn. Finally, if @code{@@names} is anything else, just let it get
turned into a string automatically and do the default greeting.

Let's look at that iterator in more depth:

@verbatim
@names.each do |name|
  puts "Hello #{name}!"
end
@end verbatim

@code{each} is a method that accepts a block of code then runs that
block of code for every element in a list, and the bit between @code{do}
and @code{end} is just such a block. A block is like an anonymous
function or @code{lambda}. The variable between pipe characters is the
parameter for this block.

What happens here is that for every entry in a list, @code{name} is
bound to that list element, and then the expression
@code{puts "Hello #@{name@}!"} is run with that name.

Most other programming languages handle going over a list using the
@code{for} loop, which in C looks something like:

@verbatim
for (i=0; i<number_of_elements; i++)
{
  do_something_with(element[i]);
}
@end verbatim

This works, but isn't very elegant. You need a throw-away variable like
@code{i}, have to figure out how long the list is, and have to explain
how to walk over the list. The Ruby way is much more elegant, all the
housekeeping details are hidden within the @code{each} method, all you
need to do is to tell it what to do with each element. Internally, the
@code{each} method will essentially call @code{yield "Albert"}, then
@code{yield "Brenda"} and then @code{yield "Charles"}, and so on.

@node Blocks the Highly Sparkling Glint on the Edge of Ruby
@section Blocks, the Highly Sparkling Glint on the Edge of Ruby

The real power of blocks is when dealing with things that are more
complicated than lists. Beyond handling simple housekeeping details
within the method, you can also handle setup, teardown, and errors---all
hidden away from the cares of the user.

@verbatim
# Say bye to everybody
def say_bye
  if @names.nil?
    puts "..."
  elsif @names.respond_to?("join")
    # Join the list elements with commas
    puts "Goodbye #{@names.join(", ")}.  Come back soon!"
  else
    puts "Goodbye #{@names}.  Come back soon!"
  end
end
@end verbatim

The @code{say_bye} method doesn't use @code{each}, instead it checks to
see if @code{@@names} responds to the @code{join} method, and if so,
uses it. Otherwise, it just prints out the variable as a string. This
method of not caring about the actual @emph{type} of a variable, just
relying on what methods it supports is known as ``Duck Typing'', as in
``if it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck@dots{}''. The benefit
of this is that it doesn't unnecessarily restrict the types of variables
that are supported. If someone comes up with a new kind of list class,
as long as it implements the @code{join} method with the same semantics
as other lists, everything will work as planned.

@node Kicking Off the Script
@section Kicking Off the Script

So, that's the MegaGreeter class, the rest of the file just calls
methods on that class. There's one final trick to notice, and that's the
line:

@verbatim
if __FILE__ == $0
@end verbatim

@code{__FILE__} is the magic variable that contains the name of the
current file. @code{$0} is the name of the file used to start the
program. This check says ``If this is the main file being used@dots{}''
This allows a file to be used as a library, and not to execute code in
that context, but if the file is being used as an executable, then
execute that code.

@node Consider Yourself Introduced
@section Consider Yourself Introduced

So that's it for the quick tour of Ruby. There's a lot more to explore,
the different control structures that Ruby offers; the use of blocks and
@code{yield}; modules as mixins; and more. I hope this taste of Ruby has
left you wanting to learn more.
